# Design. Every node here is a proposal drawn from the ground (hub 1) and the evidence (hubs 3 and 4).
# A decision names what would flip it and the open questions (hub 6) it waits on.

n: 5.1
name: node-space
kind: hub
is: One space of immutable nodes is the root. A node is a blueprint and ordered children, or a leaf of bytes. Recipes, blueprints, cells, grammar rules and lenses are all nodes of this one shape, and the stored node is the thing that runs.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.1.1
name: one-node-shape
kind: decision
is: A node is a blueprint id and an ordered list of child ids, or a blueprint id and a leaf of bytes. Nothing else is in a node: no name, no position in a text, no state, no table pointer.
status: inferred
from: 1.9.2 3.1.8.2 3.7.8.2 3.1.2.5
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.1.2
name: stored-node-is-what-runs
kind: decision
is: The walker and the emitter read the same nodes that are stored and shared. There is no second, private tree for execution and no internal bytecode between the stored form and the interpreter.
status: inferred
from: 3.4.2.4 4.2.8.3 4.2.1.6 3.7.2.11
conf: high
why: The old body kept recipes twice, as node trees for data and as a private tagged tree for running, and the two drifted.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.1.3
name: blueprint-recipe-cell
kind: term
is: A blueprint is a node that describes a shape or an interface. A recipe is a node that composes other nodes and can be offered. A cell is a holder: a name bound to a present node, declared with a base blueprint, an access recipe and a constructor recipe. All three are nodes; they differ by blueprint, not by representation.
status: inferred
from: 1.7.1 1.2 3.1.2.8 3.2.8.1 3.8.8.1 3.8.8.7 3.8.4.5
conf: mid
limit: NUMS.Go's cell is a declared slot with no run-time instance; a run-time cell that holds a value has no working reference in any source read.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.1.4
name: leaves-are-their-own-content
kind: decision
is: A number is a leaf whose bytes are its value, so equal numbers are one node with no table between. Width, signedness and layout belong to the leaf's blueprint; an 8-bit and a 64-bit integer are different blueprints.
status: inferred
from: 3.4.8.2 3.1.2.4 3.5.8.8
conf: mid
open: 6.1.11
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.1.5
name: seeded-catalogue-and-namespaces
kind: decision
is: The bootstrap seeds a small fixed catalogue: the leaf blueprints and about 25 core operations. Every other blueprint is minted. Blueprints live in namespaces by altitude and by host language, and a higher namespace lowers into a lower one inside the same space.
status: inferred
from: 3.1.8.1 3.1.8.5 4.4.8.3 4.4.1.8 3.13
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2
name: identity
kind: hub
is: Three identities are kept apart: a content id computed from a node's canonical bytes, a 32-bit handle used only in memory, and a name that can move. Identity is computed when a node freezes or crosses a boundary. Names sit in a table beside the nodes, one column per lens.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.1
name: three-identities
kind: decision
is: A content id says what a node is and is the same in every runtime. A handle is a 32-bit index into one process's store and never leaves it. A name is a binding from a lens to a node and is the only thing that moves. No one of the three stands in for another.
status: inferred
from: 4.4.8.5 3.4.2.3 3.7.2.9 3.15 1.9.3
conf: high
why: Both references use arrival-order numbers as identity: NUMS.Go's ids differ from run to run, and the old body's composite id is an index into a host-wide field.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.2
name: content-id
kind: decision
is: The content id is SHA-256 over the node's canonical encoding: its blueprint id, then its children named by their content ids, or its leaf bytes. No name enters it. All 256 bits are stored once per frozen node.
status: inferred
from: 3.15.2.2 3.15.2.3 3.15.4.4 3.15.8.1 3.15.8.2 4.4.8.1 4.5.3.1 3.13.8.2 1.3
conf: mid
why: Measured on the first machine: 16 ns per 40-byte node through the CPU's SHA instructions, against 97 ns for the system library call. 64 bits collide with probability 0.39 at 2^32 nodes.
limit: SHA-256 is this node's own choice, standing on measurements.
flip: A target without SHA instructions, or very large leaves, would favour BLAKE3; neither case was measured.
open: 6.1.18 6.1.14 6.1.4 6.1.31
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.3
name: identity-at-freeze
kind: decision
is: A content id is computed when a node freezes or is offered across a boundary, not when it is made. A transient node is bump-allocated with no id.
status: inferred
from: 3.15.8.4 3.15.4.1 3.15.2.1 3.15.3.2 4.5.8.7 4.5.9.4
conf: mid
why: Measured: a bump allocation of a 40-byte node costs 1.2 to 2.3 ns; minting with a stored cryptographic digest costs 40 to 45 ns, which at ten million nodes a second is 45 percent of a core.
open: 6.1.25
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.4
name: names-beside-nodes
kind: decision
is: Names are rows in a table keyed by node and lens, zero or more per node, and a lens is itself a node. Names a person chooses live in the same table as keywords. Bound variables are referred to by position, so renaming changes no node.
status: inferred
from: 3.1.3.2 3.1.8.3 4.4.8.2 4.4.1.5 1.7.18 1.7.2
conf: high
why: Run twice: renaming every identifier in a file left NUMS.Go's node databases identical.
limit: Every system read that swaps labels across human languages stops at user-chosen names and at strings inside data.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.5
name: values-inside-shape-beside
kind: decision
is: A literal value is a leaf inside the node, so a recipe's content id covers what it computes with. The recipe's shape, with every literal replaced by a hole, is a second node derived from it and used as a fingerprint and as the key for sharing compiled code.
status: inferred
from: 3.1.3.3 3.2.8.6 4.4.4.5
conf: mid
why: NUMS.Go keeps literal values outside the tree, which makes 'a + 1' and 'b + 2' one node: the id then addresses a shape and not a meaning.
open: 6.1.5
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.6
name: four-part-reading
kind: decision
is: The axiom file's line (package level type instance) is kept as a reading of any node, each part derived from content: package is the namespace of the node's blueprint, level is its height, type is its blueprint, instance is its content id, or its value for a leaf. No part is handed out by arrival order.
status: inferred
from: 1.2 3.1.2.1 3.1.2.2 3.1.9.1 3.4.2.2 3.3.9.4 3.2.9.1
conf: low
why: NUMS.Go and the old runtime share the four field names and disagree on what Level, Package and Type mean; the line sits inside axiom 2's block of the acknowledged file.
open: 6.1.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.2.7
name: holder-identity
kind: decision
is: A thing that changes over time is a holder: a node minted once with a unique leaf, and a binding from it to its present content. The holder's id stays; each state it passes through is its own immutable node.
status: inferred
from: 1.9.3 3.12.5.3 3.3.8.4
conf: mid
why: The object model needs a first id that stays put while content changes, and a content hash cannot be that id.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3
name: grammar
kind: hub
is: One grammar, one reader. A lens is a set of rows that one interpreter reads forwards to make nodes and backwards to print them. Reading text is a choice that may find no node or several; printing is total from a chosen node. Host languages and human languages enter as lenses and namespaces, not as a universal set of constructs.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.1
name: one-grammar-one-reader
kind: decision
is: There is one grammar and one reader. Text becomes nodes in one step, and no stage hands text to another stage. No second dialect exists for a lower altitude.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.2.1 3.5.9.3 3.5.9.4 3.3.9.1 3.4.9.1 3.5.1.3 3.13
conf: high
why: The old body carried three readers that gave fail, cut, choice, class and template different meanings, and lowered one grammar to the other through text.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.2
name: lens-is-rows
kind: decision
is: A lens is data: rule rows of name, pattern and template, an operator table, and lexicon rows that map neutral leaves to this lens's words. One interpreter reads a row forwards to build a node and runs the same template backwards to print one, so no printer is written by hand.
status: inferred
from: 3.11.4.2 3.11.4.3 3.11.4.4 3.11.4.5 3.11.4.7 3.11.8.1 3.2.8.2 3.2.8.3 3.5.8.7 3.4.8.6 3.1.4.3
conf: mid
why: A 360-line probe read one typed method from Go, Java and Python text to the same node and printed it back through each lens.
limit: The probe covers about 15 constructs and keeps names inside nodes; nothing measures the cost for a whole language.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.3
name: reading-is-a-choice
kind: decision
is: Reading text through a lens answers with no node, one node or several candidates. Printing a chosen node through a lens is total over the blueprints the lens covers; for a blueprint it has no row for, the lens declines by name before printing anything.
status: inferred
from: 4.4.8.2 4.4.9.3 3.10 3.11 4.4.8.6
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.4
name: canonical-print
kind: decision
is: Every node has one generic printed form of numbers and brackets that always reads back to the same node. It is the serialization the bootstrap reads. It has no sugar and no semantics of its own, and nobody writes in it except to bring the first seed into being.
status: inferred
from: 4.4.1.8 3.13 3.4.2.7
conf: mid
open: 6.2.5 6.1.10
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.5
name: home-lens
kind: decision
is: The surface people write in is one lens among others, defined by rows written in itself. It carries everything the object and control model can express, at the altitude of the compact 2026 surface: expression-bodied definitions, lambdas, pipelines, match, blueprint literals.
status: assumed
see: 3.5.8.6 3.5.8.7
limit: Its syntax is not designed.
open: 6.1.17
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.6
name: host-languages-are-namespaces
kind: decision
is: A host programming language enters as its own namespace of blueprints plus a lens. Two languages share a node only where both lenses map to the same neutral blueprint, and an equivalence between two language-specific blueprints is a separate recorded relation with its witness.
status: inferred
from: 4.4.8.4 4.4.9.1 4.4.1.7 3.11.1.4 3.11.4.10 3.11.9.1 3.2.3.5 3.2.9.3
conf: mid
why: Every project read that tried one shared construct set for many languages abandoned or narrowed it.
limit: Shared identity is only as true as the lexicon rows: a row that says int64, long and Python int are one leaf is false for overflow.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.7
name: human-languages-come-last
kind: decision
is: A human language is a lens whose rows linearise concept nodes, with lexeme mappings that carry context, provenance and a review count. It is built after the kernel compiles itself and is kept separable, so the kernel does not depend on the claim that all human languages share one root.
status: inferred
from: 3.10.1.8 3.10.2.6 3.10.8.2 3.10.8.3 4.4.8.8 4.4.4.3 4.4.2.3 4.4.2.1
conf: mid
open: 6.1.19
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.3.8
name: unresolved-is-a-fault
kind: decision
is: A name that does not resolve, or text no rule can read, is a reported fault with its place. It is never turned into nothing and never into a value.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.2.5 3.7.9.4 3.1.9.4 3.7.2.6 3.7.8.1
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4
name: acknowledgment-and-control
kind: hub
is: Every evaluation is an offer acknowledged by nothing, 0, 1 or a node. Control is built from ordered choice, one delimiter that commits, fail and hold; the rest is derived. Fail is not a value: it reverses every change back to the nearest open choice and runs that choice's next option. Arguments are evaluated once and in order. Each state declares how it is undone.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.1
name: ack-is-the-evaluation-rule
kind: decision
is: Evaluating any node is an offer, and its result is exactly one of nothing, 0, 1 or a node. A call, a message, a host call and a request over a wire use this one rule.
status: inferred
from: 1.9.5 4.5.8.1 3.3.8.2 3.4.8.1
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.2
name: fail-is-backtracking
kind: decision
is: Fail is not a value. A fail means backtrack: every change made since the nearest open choice is reversed, newest first, and that choice's next option runs forward from the state the choice was entered with, as BML defines it. When every option of a choice has failed, the offer receives nothing.
status: given
see: 3.5.2.2 3.5.2.4 3.5.2.6 5.4.7 5.4.8 5.4.9
why: This is the 2000 machine's reading, where failure is a mode of the machine and undo is what the machine does in it.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.3
name: choice-moves-on-a-fail
kind: decision
is: An ordered choice tries its next option when the option before it fails, after that option's changes are undone. A 0 is an answer and does not move a choice on. The reason a failed option leaves is exempt from the undo and is handed to the next option, and the record of the choice counts failed and timed-out options apart.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.2 5.4.4 1.9.8 1.7.19 3.7.3.1
conf: mid
limit: The 2000 machine exempted state from undo by an attribute on a variable or stream; that a reason node is exempt by rule is this design's choice.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.13
name: values-after-fail
kind: decision
is: The four acknowledgments read: a node is an answer that carries something; 1 is yes with nothing to carry; 0 is an answer of no that the recipe returns and stands by, its changes kept; nothing is what an offer receives when no answer stands, either because every path failed and was undone or because no answer arrived in time. The reason beside the acknowledgment says which.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.2 5.4.4 1.7.17 1.7.19 3.3.4.2 3.3.4.3
conf: high
see: 3.3.4.1 3.3.4.10 4.5.1.3
limit: That 0 is an answer a recipe stands by, with its changes kept, is this design's inference.
why: A path that failed has been reversed, so nothing of it stands: the axioms file and the old running core read fail as a silence for this reason. 'Said no' and 'no path survived' stay apart in the value; 'no path survived' and 'timed out' stay apart in the reason.
open: 6.1.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.4
name: reason-beside-the-ack
kind: decision
is: An outcome is the acknowledgment plus an optional reason node beside it. The reason says which silence or which no this was: timed out, not yet, declined, never existed. The set of acknowledgments stays at four.
status: inferred
from: 4.4.8.7 3.3.4.9 3.5.8.4 4.4.3.2 3.13
conf: mid
why: One marker that carries several readings, with each operator choosing its own rule, is how SQL NULL failed; the old body's nothing carried at least seven readings.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.5
name: fault-is-not-an-acknowledgment
kind: decision
is: A fault is a broken promise of the system itself: an unresolved name, a malformed node, an operation applied to the wrong kind. It unwinds to the nearest recover point with a reason node and is counted apart from silence and from no.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.8.1 3.4.4.3 3.7.3.3 3.3.4.4 5.3.8
conf: mid
open: 6.1.21
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.6
name: control-primitives
kind: decision
is: The control primitives are: offer, ordered choice, one delimiter that commits a choice (taking one answer or all), fail, hold, and one recover point. If, not, every, try, cut, once, defer, retry and timeout are written over these and are not built in.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.1.10 4.5.8.2 3.5.5 3.5.6 3.5.8.1 3.4.4.2 3.7.8.3
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.7
name: two-strengths-of-choice
kind: decision
is: The general choice is re-entrant: a later fail returns into the next option of a choice that already answered, until a commit closes it. The everyday form, first option that answers, is that choice with a commit at its exit.
status: inferred
from: 3.5.8.2 3.5.2.2 3.5.2.3 3.5.4.2
conf: mid
why: In the 2000 sources the whole grammar engine, branch and repeat with back-off, is a few lines of re-entrant choose and fail. Every 2026 reader implements only the commit-at-exit form.
open: 6.1.7
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.8
name: reversibility-is-declared
kind: decision
is: Each piece of state declares how it is undone: repointable (immutable content; undo moves a name back), journaled (a live cell; old values go on a trail), has-inverse (a pair of do and undo recipes), or irreversible (it crossed the system's boundary). An irreversible effect inside an open choice is refused unless it is buffered until the commit.
status: inferred
from: 3.5.8.3 4.5.8.3 4.5.1.4 3.5.2.7 3.5.2.10 1.7.8
conf: mid
open: 6.1.15
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.9
name: choice-point-stack
kind: decision
is: At machine level a choice is a choice-point frame that saves registers, a trail mark and an allocation mark, plus a trail that records old values only for cells older than the choice point. An alternative is re-entered from the saved frame; no stack is copied and no continuation is captured.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.1.1 4.5.9.1 4.5.1.8 4.5.8.3
conf: mid
limit: The costs are word counts from published layouts, not measurements.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.10
name: determinism-on-the-recipe
kind: decision
is: Each recipe carries an inferred determinism: exactly one answer, at most one, at least one, or any number. A recipe with exactly one answer compiles with no choice machinery.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.1.6 4.5.8.4
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.11
name: timeout-is-an-ack
kind: decision
is: An offer may carry a deadline. When it passes, the offer acknowledges nothing with the reason 'timed out', and no fault is raised. Waiting is by event, never by polling.
status: inferred
from: 1.9.6 4.5.3.8 3.7.4.7 3.5.2.8
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.4.12
name: strict-order-in-the-node-form
kind: decision
is: Arguments are evaluated once, left to right, and bound before the call. This is a property of the node form every tier reads, so no tier can evaluate an argument twice or skip its effect.
status: inferred
from: 3.9.8.5 3.9.4.4 3.7.4.3 3.7.2.2 3.6.3.6
conf: high
why: The old body's recorded wrong answers under heat came from a fast path that inlined by name and dropped effects.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5
name: objects-and-generics
kind: hub
is: A reference is a pair of ids, the cell and the view through which it is seen. Several parents mean one composed shape, so an activation carries one cell and the view of it that the running recipe may read. A generic is a recipe from blueprints to blueprints, and specialised code is a cache.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.1
name: reference-is-a-pair
kind: decision
is: A reference is two ids, a cell and a view, or an immediate value. No table pointer sits inside the cell's content, so a cell's content id does not depend on how it is viewed, and a second interface over the same data needs no wrapper.
status: inferred
from: 3.12.1.1 3.12.4.1 3.12.8.1 3.5.3.4 4.5.8.5 4.5.2.1 4.5.9.2 3.5.8.5
conf: mid
why: The 2000 machine's logs count interface casts and interface-forwarded calls by the thousand; this is the one object-model element with a record of running.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.2
name: self-and-this
kind: decision
is: An activation carries one reference: the cell the message was sent to, and the view of it whose slots the running recipe may read. With parents composed into one shape (5.5.4), self and this name the same cell and differ only in the view. Dispatch follows five rules: send, self-send, super (the view moves to the parent's part of the shape), slot access only through the view, and cast. The reference is saved whole on every choice point and never rebuilt.
status: inferred
from: 5.5.4 5.5.1 3.12.1.3 3.12.8.2 3.12.4.2 3.12.4.5 3.12.1.6 3.12.9.4 3.5.8.5
conf: mid
limit: The 2000 machine let self and this be different objects; its instructions for that appear in no run log, and the 2026 fixtures for it were deleted on 2026-09-29. This design does not include it.
open: 6.2.4
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.3
name: views-are-derived
kind: decision
is: A view of a shape through an interface is computed on demand and remembered under the pair (interface id, shape id). A cast is a lookup in that memory. A shape conforms to an interface by structure, so conformance can be added after the shape exists.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.8.5 4.5.2.2 3.12.8.3
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.4
name: several-parents
kind: decision
is: A blueprint with several parents means one composed shape: one cell that holds the slots of every parent. Delegation to a separate cell is not what a parent means; it stays writable as a slot that holds another cell and a recipe that forwards to it.
status: given
see: 3.12.2.4 3.12.8.5 3.12.4.8 3.12.5.2 4.5.8.6 4.5.2.4 4.5.2.5 3.5.3.3 5.5.7
why: This is what the 2026 code does and the one reading with a record of running; separate base objects with self kept while this moves have no recorded execution in either era.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.7
name: composing-parents
kind: decision
is: Composing is done once, when the blueprint is made: an ancestor reached through two parents is held once, and a name that two parents both define is resolved in the text, the system declining to guess. The composed blueprint is a node like any other, so its content id fixes the layout.
status: inferred
from: 5.5.4 3.12.5.2 4.5.2.4 4.5.2.5
conf: mid
limit: That a shared ancestor is held once is this design's choice; 5.5.4 does not say it.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.5
name: generics-are-recipes-over-blueprints
kind: decision
is: A template is a recipe that takes blueprints and answers a blueprint or a recipe. An instance is identified by the template's id and its arguments' ids. Passing a table of operations is the meaning; code specialised for one set of arguments is a cache under that identity.
status: inferred
from: 3.12.3.1 3.12.3.2 3.12.8.6 4.5.8.6 4.5.2.6 3.5.3.1 3.5.8.6
conf: mid
why: Specialisation by substitution is the one generic mechanism with a record in both eras.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.5.6
name: attributes
kind: decision
is: Any declaration can carry attributes, and programs can define new ones. An attribute with no defined behaviour is carried and reported, never dropped. Visibility, embedding and constness sit on the reference to a blueprint, not on the blueprint.
status: inferred
from: 3.5.8.6 3.5.3.6 3.1.2.4 3.1.8.4
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6
name: node-states
kind: hub
is: Ice, water and gas are states of a node and are independent of its kind. Ice is the small essential part that must persist; water is a held working form; gas is transient and derivable. Only ice is stored in git. Compiled machine code is never essential ice.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6.1
name: state-is-not-kind
kind: decision
is: A node's state is separate from whether it is a blueprint, a recipe or a cell. Any kind can be in any state.
status: inferred
from: 3.14.3.3 3.14.3.2 3.8.3.4 3.8.3.5
conf: high
why: Blueprints exist in water and gas as well, and recipes in all three states (3.14.3.3).
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6.2
name: ice-water-gas
kind: term
is: Ice is the small essential part that must persist; water is a held working form; gas is transient and derivable; each is a state of any node. Read further: ice is immutable, identified by content and shareable as it is; water is held and changeable by one holder and lost with the holder unless frozen; gas is private, in memory and discardable because it can be derived again.
status: given
see: 3.14.1.1 3.14.1.3 3.14.3.6 3.14.8.2
why: It agrees with the oldest definition on disk, from September 2025: ice is essential persisted facts, water is materialized caches, gas is derivable and transient; only ice needs to persist.
limit: The reading after the first sentence is this design's own.
open: 6.1.29
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6.3
name: keep-ice-tiny
kind: decision
is: Only what cannot be derived again is ice. The repository holds only ice: the bootstrap, the seed's meaning, the axioms and this graph. Every other byte is a lease that can be dropped and remade.
status: inferred
from: 3.14.1.2 3.14.3.1 3.3.3.1 4.2.3.5
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6.4
name: compiled-code-is-gas-but-the-seed
kind: decision
is: Machine code is a materialization of a recipe for one target. It lives as gas in memory and may be kept as water in a cache keyed by recipe id, generator id and target id; losing it loses nothing. The one exception is the seed: the leaves a process holds before anything is walked, which only compiled code can be, are ice, kept in git, small, and made again by the seed itself the same bytes.
status: inferred
from: 3.14.4.3 3.14.1.3 3.14.3.1 3.14.2.6 3.6.9.1 3.6.2.6 3.6.3.3 3.7.8.6 4.2.8.6
conf: high
why: The 2025 definition says a compiled library is gas. The 2026 body drifted to calling its compiled image ice, and that image grew to 645 times its source. A piece that leaves C must already be compiled when a process starts, since the compiler runs on it, so what the seed carries cannot be gas.
limit: Holds while the seed carries only floor leaves, which hold no address and read only the catalogue's fixed handles; on 2026-11-28 it carries the restore of a choice, 16,408 bytes of AArch64.
at: 2026-11-28

n: 5.6.5
name: state-invariants
kind: decision
is: Three rules can be checked mechanically. Only water changes in place. A node refers only to nodes at least as committed as itself, so ice never points at water or gas. A change of state keeps the node and its kind.
status: inferred
from: 3.14.8.3 3.14.8.4 3.14.8.5 5.6.2
conf: mid
limit: These rules are a proposal; no source states them and nothing enforces them yet.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6.6
name: transitions-are-operations
kind: decision
is: State changes are named operations: freeze (water to ice; computes the content id and needs an acknowledged offer), melt (ice to a working copy in water), condense and evaporate (between gas and water), sublimate (release the bytes, keep the id), thaw (read ice back and verify its id). Counts of reads, changes and sharing invite a transition and never perform it.
status: inferred
from: 3.14.8.7 3.14.8.8 3.13.2.8 5.2.3
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.6.7
name: state-decides-undo
kind: finding
is: The state of a piece of data gives its reversibility class with no further declaration: ice is undone by repointing a name, water by a journal of old values, gas by discarding it. Only effects that left the system need an explicit rule.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.8 5.6.2 3.5.2.10
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.7
name: store-and-memory
kind: hub
is: All working memory is one shared region, the framebuffer, that a second process can record and replay at no call or wait on the runtime's side. Within it water and gas are allocated in tables a choice point can release in one step, and every record starts with its blueprint and its last writer. Ice is a file read into it. A value's kind is read from its blueprint and never guessed from the bits.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.7.1
name: ice-store
kind: decision
is: Frozen nodes live in a compact store that is mapped into memory and read where it lies, with children named by id. Because a child must exist before its parent, the store has no cycles, and counting references reclaims everything that nothing holds. A write is a temporary file renamed into place.
status: inferred
from: 4.2.3.7 4.5.3.2 4.5.3.4 4.5.8.7 3.7.4.1 4.4.9.5
conf: mid
open: 6.1.14
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.7.2
name: regions-follow-choice
kind: decision
is: Transient nodes are allocated by moving a pointer in a region. A choice point records the pointer; backing out of an option moves it back, releasing everything that option made.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.5 4.2.3.7 5.4.9
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.7.3
name: handles-in-memory
kind: decision
is: In memory a node is referred to by a 32-bit handle, a place in its process's lane of the substrate; among the members of a named substrate the handle of a settled node names it in each. The digest is stored once with the frozen node and is used only at freeze, thaw and boundary crossings. A cheap non-cryptographic hash may pick a slot in a table as long as equality is decided by the bytes.
status: inferred
from: 3.15.8.3 3.15.3.4 3.15.3.7 3.13.8.2
conf: mid
why: Measured: a 32-byte digest costs 120 bytes per interned node against 56 with none, and a slow digest in front of a table probe raised the probe from about 20 ns to about 90 ns.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.7.4
name: kinds-are-explicit
kind: decision
is: What a value is comes from its blueprint. No operation infers a kind from a number's parity, size or range, and every primitive is defined for every kind it can meet, answering nothing or a fault where it has no meaning.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.2.4 3.6.9.4 3.7.9.3 3.4.9.5
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.7.5
name: no-capacities-in-meaning
kind: decision
is: No limit on counts or sizes is part of the language's meaning. A bound that exists for a machine reason is a named resource that answers nothing with a reason when it runs out.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.2.14 3.13
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.7.6
name: framebuffer
kind: decision
is: All memory of every object, node and stack is read and written in one shared space that can be recorded without any effect on the run, as video, hence framebuffer, and played back to see what happened when and where. The framebuffer is the runtime's working memory itself, not a channel the runtime writes records into.
status: given
see: 5.7.7 5.7.8 5.8.6 5.14.10 5.14.11
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.7.7
name: framebuffer-describes-itself
kind: decision
is: Beside each cell or object stand its blueprint and type, and a pointer to the source of its last writer at the smallest footprint, so diagnostics are live and cost the runtime nothing.
status: given
see: 5.7.6 5.7.4
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.7.8
name: framebuffer-reading
kind: decision
is: This design reads 5.7.6 and 5.7.7 as five rules, fixed in spec/framebuffer.md. One region is reserved once and shared, and nothing the runtime keeps lives outside it and the substrate its nodes lie in (5.14.61). Every record starts with the handle of its blueprint and the handle of the program node that last wrote it. A reference is a handle or an offset, never an address, so a copy reads the same anywhere and later. A record is complete before the count that makes it reachable moves past it. The runtime calls nothing and waits for nothing on behalf of an observer.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.6 5.7.7 5.7.3
src: 2.30.9
conf: mid
limit: Values held only in machine registers are seen once stored. Executable pages and the C library's own memory lie outside the region (5.14.12).
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.8
name: boundary-and-host
kind: hub
is: The host is reached through one table of host functions, each of which the system can later replace. A port has a direction, a shape and a name. What crosses a boundary is one structured outcome. Cells run inside the process and are watched by a second process that reads the framebuffer.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.8.1
name: host-table
kind: decision
is: Every host capability is one function with the C calling convention in a table handed to emitted code: reserve the shared region, map a code region, begin and end writing code for this thread, flush the instruction cache, read a file, look up a symbol, make a raw call, start a thread, read a clock. Each entry is a unit the system can replace with code it compiled.
status: inferred
from: 4.1.8.2 4.1.8.5 3.3.2.1 3.3.2.2 3.9
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.8.2
name: typed-ports
kind: decision
is: A port is a direction, a value shape and a name. Offering to a name nobody holds acknowledges nothing; offering the wrong shape acknowledges 0 and leaves a record; otherwise the holder's own acknowledgment comes back. Offers and silences are counted per port.
status: inferred
from: 3.13.2.6 3.13.8.4 3.3.2.1 3.3.8.6
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.8.3
name: frameworks-through-one-trampoline
kind: decision
is: Host frameworks are reached by looking up a symbol and calling it through one trampoline, the same way for Metal as for the C library. No adapter is written per framework.
status: inferred
from: 4.1.4.2 3.6.9.5 4.1.1.7
conf: mid
why: Measured: Metal reached from plain C in about 20 lines at 5.9 ns per message.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.8.4
name: references-are-capabilities
kind: decision
is: Holding a reference to a cell through a view is the right to offer to it through that view, and there is no other way in. Nothing in the system has ambient authority over another cell. A crossing that did not go through an offered view leaves a record.
status: inferred
from: 4.5.8.8 4.5.3.5 1.9.4 5.5.1
conf: mid
limit: A content id of known content is not a secret, so reading by id cannot be what protects a cell's interior.
open: 6.1.9
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.8.5
name: one-structured-outcome
kind: decision
is: What comes back across a boundary is one value: the acknowledgment, the reason beside it, and what it cost in time. No part of the system reads another part's printed last line or exit code.
status: inferred
from: 3.4.1.7 3.7.2.6 3.7.9.6 3.4.8.4
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.8.6
name: cells-in-process
kind: decision
is: A unit of work runs as a cell inside the process with its own deadline. The system does not start a copy of itself to run something. A cell is watched by reading its memory in the framebuffer from another process; it emits nothing for a watcher.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.4.7 3.7.2.11 3.7.8.5 5.7.6
conf: mid
open: 6.1.12
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9
name: bootstrap
kind: hub
is: The stored bootstrap is one C file whose lines the build counts. It holds the framebuffer, a node store, a reader of stored records, a walker of about 25 operations whose one job is to run the compiler once, the birth of executable pages, and the host table. Everything else is written in the one grammar and compiled by the system.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.1
name: bootstrap-in-c
kind: decision
is: The bootstrap is written in C: a strict C99 subset, one translation unit, no library use beyond a declared list of operating-system symbols.
status: inferred
from: 4.1.8.1 3.6.4.5 4.2.8.2 4.1.2.3 4.1.2.4 4.1.2.5 4.1.4.1 4.1.4.7 4.1.3.3
conf: high
why: Measured on the first machine: a C build is 34 KB in 0.05 s. A Go collection waited 627 ms behind a goroutine sitting in emitted code, and Go's maintainers do not support foreign frames. Rust can do it; its safety helps where input is parsed, not in the emitter, which is unsafe in any language.
alt: Go: its runtime is a second kernel that cannot be replaced piece by piece. Rust: capable, larger toolchain, its emitter libraries assume an operating system. Zig: before 1.0, with breaking changes each release. Hand-written machine code: macOS needs a signed, dynamically linked image.
flip: Rust if the bootstrap must stay above 10,000 lines and parse untrusted input. Go if it gains supported foreign frames. Zig after 1.0. A chain from raw bytes if auditing from bytes becomes the first criterion.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.2
name: line-budget
kind: decision
is: The lines of C in the bootstrap are counted at every build, and no fixed number gates it. The direction is down: each part that can be written in Form leaves C, held to the C it replaces, until only what Form cannot start or reach the host without remains.
status: inferred
from: 4.1.8.4 3.6.4.3 3.6.4.4 3.13.3.4 3.13.1.2 3.7.3.2 3.3.4.5 3.6.1.4
conf: mid
why: The old seed grew from 160 to 34,149 lines over 400 commits under a written rule that it shrinks. Estimates for the smallest floor: 1,400 to 1,600 lines in one study, 1,800 to 2,200 with a walker in another.
limit: The one comparable experiment, the rewrite of 2026-11-06, went from 1,574 to 10,302 lines in 56 hours.
open: 6.2.6
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.3
name: bootstrap-contents
kind: decision
is: The bootstrap holds exactly: process entry; the one shared region that is all of its memory; a store that interns, freezes and thaws nodes; a reader of stored records; a walker over the seeded core operations; the birth of executable pages; the host table with a symbol lookup and one call trampoline; a fault handler that records and does not resume.
status: inferred
from: 3.6.4.4 3.13.3.3 3.13.3.4 3.9.3.5 3.6.8.3
conf: mid
open: 6.2.2
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.4
name: executable-memory-contract
kind: decision
is: From the first line the bootstrap follows the Apple silicon rules: one code region reserved once with MAP_JIT, a per-thread switch around each write, an instruction-cache flush after it, and correct behaviour both unsigned and under the hardened runtime with the allow-jit entitlement.
status: inferred
from: 4.1.1.1 4.1.1.3 4.1.1.4 4.1.1.5 4.1.8.3 3.6.2.1 4.2.4.2
conf: high
why: Measured: the per-thread switch costs about 21 ns a pair; changing page protection costs about 454 ns and is killed under the hardened runtime.
limit: Whether the documented one-region limit is enforced on the current macOS was not tested. Other hosts were read, not run.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.5
name: seed
kind: decision
is: The compiler, the home lens and the emitter are written in the one grammar. The repository keeps them as text under the home lens, which is what people review, and as a canonical-print seed, which is what the bootstrap reads on a cold start. The running system regenerates the seed from the text and it must match byte for byte.
status: inferred
from: 4.2.3.1 4.1.4.7 4.2.8.1 3.6.4.3 5.3.4
conf: low
limit: The first seed has to be written directly in the canonical print, before any home lens can read itself.
open: 6.1.10
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.6
name: self-replacement
kind: decision
is: The system replaces its bootstrap one piece at a time: it compiles the piece from Form, the seed carries it, and the C of that piece is deleted in the same change. The seed is the cold start: every process runs it before anything is walked. What a person audits is the seed's nodes through the lens and the fixpoint, the seed built on itself giving the same bytes. The last step is writing its own signed executable image.
status: inferred
from: 1.9.7 4.1.8.5 4.2.3.1 3.9 3.6.8.6
conf: mid
limit: The old body's own object-file writer produces an unsigned object with one section; a signed executable written by the system has no working reference. A leaf shows through the lens as bytes until a lens reads instructions back. A lost seed is taken from git history, or built by the last commit whose C walker restores a fail alone.
at: 2026-11-28

n: 5.9.7
name: authoring-test
kind: decision
is: The bootstrap is accepted when the system is made of nothing it cannot also write: every piece of the C file has a counterpart in the grammar that the system has compiled and run in its place.
status: inferred
from: 3.3.2.4 3.3.2.5
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.9.8
name: new-floor-new-measure
kind: decision
is: The new floor is judged by its own small set of laws and by the conformance vectors of its node encoding. It is not asked to pass the old body's checks, carry its registry of primitives, or read its surfaces.
status: inferred
from: 3.13.1.8 3.13.1.2 3.13.1.4 3.13.9.1 3.7.2.10
conf: high
why: The rewrite of 2026-11-06 grew sixfold in 56 hours chasing the old body's 124-name registry and its two surfaces, and stopped at an evaluator 40 times slower.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10
name: execution-tiers
kind: hub
is: Speed comes in tiers that never change meaning: a walker for the cold start, a one-pass emitter written in the grammar, then an optimizing tier, then matrix and GPU backends. One definition per operation feeds every tier. The unit that is compiled and cached is the recipe.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.1
name: heat-never-changes-meaning
kind: decision
is: Each operation has one definition. The walker's arm calls it, and compiled code calls it on its slow path; compiled code adds only a fast path behind a one-instruction test of kind. No second implementation of an operation exists for speed.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.8.4 3.9.8.3 3.9.5.3 3.9.5.5 3.7.2.2 3.6.3.6 3.6.9.2 4.2.1.4 3.7.4.3
conf: high
why: Every recorded wrong answer under heat in the old body belongs to a hand-written C fast path or a duplicated implementation; none belongs to the emitter written in the language.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.2
name: recipe-is-the-unit
kind: decision
is: Compilation and caching work per recipe, under the key of recipe content id, generator id and target id, and no cache key contains a path, a time or a spelling. The seed is the one whole image we store: the leaves the walker needs before it can walk, built by a Form program and checked by building it again on itself.
status: inferred
from: 3.6.3.3 3.6.9.1 3.7.8.6 4.2.8.6 3.7.2.8 3.6.3.4
conf: high
why: In the old body a 59 KB source became a 38 MB image and one edited definition cost a 13 s rebuild, because the cache unit was the closure. The seed holds what the walker stands on, not what programs are made of, so it stays small.
at: 2026-11-28

n: 5.10.3
name: walker
kind: decision
is: The first tier is the bootstrap's walker, which evaluates stored nodes in place with the frame layout compiled code will use. Its one job is to run the compiler once on a cold start.
status: inferred
from: 3.13 4.2.3.1 4.2.8.1 4.2.1.6
conf: mid
open: 6.1.13
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.4
name: baseline-emitter
kind: decision
is: The second tier is an emitter written in the grammar: one pass, no intermediate form, AArch64 first. It compiles a node kind it knows and hands any other node to the walker's definition through a numbered helper table, so it can begin with ten node kinds and grow by one at a time. Live values sit in the interpreter's frame across every call out.
status: inferred
from: 3.9.8.1 3.9.8.2 3.9.8.4 3.9.2.1 3.9.5.1 3.9.5.5 4.2.1.5 4.2.8.4 3.6.8.4 3.7.4.4
conf: mid
why: The old body's 396-line emitter works this way and has no recorded wrong answer; it took a 3-million-step loop from 156 ms to 7 ms.
limit: On record that design gives about 20 times on a tight loop and 1.6 times on compiler work; whether it is fast enough as the only early tier is unmeasured.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.5
name: encodings-are-data
kind: decision
is: Instruction encodings are rows in one table used by every writer of machine code, each row checked once against an outside assembler.
status: inferred
from: 3.9.8.6 3.9.9.1 3.9.4.1 3.9.4.2
conf: high
why: The old body encodes AArch64 three separate times: decimal bases in one file, byte rows in another, and 380 hexadecimal sites in C.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.6
name: optimizing-tier
kind: decision
is: The third tier works on a control-flow graph with single-assignment values. Its rewrite rules are content-addressed recipes applied through an acyclic e-graph. Removing work comes first: guards, specialisation by kind, unboxing, constant propagation.
status: inferred
from: 4.2.8.5 4.2.1.7 4.2.2.5 4.2.2.3
conf: low
why: CPython's emitter alone gave parity; its gains came once work was removed in front of it.
open: 6.1.23
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.7
name: matrix-and-gpu
kind: decision
is: Matrix instructions and the GPU are further backends for the same lowered kernels, with the algorithm and its schedule as separate nodes. GPU programs are generated in the grammar and reached through the host table.
status: inferred
from: 4.2.8.7 4.2.4.3 4.2.4.4 5.8.3
conf: low
limit: How the old body emits GPU kernels was barely read, and nothing here is measured.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.8
name: compiled-code-can-be-refused-and-removed
kind: decision
is: A check that cold and compiled runs give the same printed values is part of the bootstrap's own self-test. A failure inside the emitter declines with a reason and leaves the recipe walked. A compiled page can be unbound.
status: inferred
from: 3.9.8.7 3.9.3.3 3.9.3.4 3.7.2.3 3.6.2.3 3.6.8.2
conf: high
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.9
name: code-holds-no-addresses
kind: decision
is: Emitted code refers to nodes and helpers through a table it is handed, never through addresses or handles written into the instructions. A page can therefore be kept as water and mapped again in another process.
status: inferred
from: 3.9.2.6 3.6.2.6 3.13.2.7
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.10
name: two-ledgers
kind: decision
is: Lines that say what the system means and lines that only make it fast are counted separately, and both counts are reported.
status: inferred
from: 4.2.3.2 4.2.8.8
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.10.11
name: no-borrowed-stencils
kind: decision
is: The first compiled tier does not use copy-and-patch with stencils made by another compiler.
status: inferred
from: 4.2.1.2 4.2.9.1 4.2.1.1
conf: mid
why: Every deployment of the technique that was read takes its stencils from LLVM at build time, which a repository holding only a seed cannot do.
flip: If the system's own optimizing tier can produce stencils good enough; nobody has shown this.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.11
name: proof-and-observation
kind: hub
is: A check counts only if it has been shown able to fail. Checks are few and say what something means. The work follows real runs end to end, watched live and replayed through the framebuffer.
at: 2026-11-25

n: 5.11.1
name: a-check-must-be-able-to-fail
kind: decision
is: A test is accepted after a planted fault has turned it red. A test of equivalence across two surfaces derives each side from its own surface. A count or a pinned number is not proof of a property.
status: inferred
from: 3.8.2.6 3.11.3.1 3.10.2.1 3.13.2.10 3.4.4.6 3.3.9.2
conf: high
why: Found in the old body: a headline equivalence test that compares a constant with itself; a surviving check titled as comparing programs that compares the integers 10 and 3; a green witness whose branch check gives both arms the same port.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.11.2
name: few-checks-that-state-meaning
kind: decision
is: Checks are counted like bootstrap lines. The laws of the core operations sit in one place and hold for the walker and for compiled code alike. A check written for one incident is folded into a law or removed.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.2.10 3.7.3.2 3.7.9.6 3.4.8.5
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.11.3
name: live-observation
kind: decision
is: Where time goes is read from outside: a second process samples the framebuffer, where the machine record names the program node running at each sample and every record names its writer. The runtime keeps no counters for a watcher. Where compiled code is absent, the reason is a node in the same memory, so each refusal is a unit of work chosen by what the samples show.
status: inferred
from: 3.7.8.5 3.6.8.5 3.9 5.7.6 7.10
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

n: 5.11.4
name: conformance-corpus
kind: plan
is: First witnesses to write: the canonical encoding's vectors, malformed ones included; the three ground programs of the old body (add, recursion, a nested numeric list); the thesis example of a class with two parents; and, as a later corpus, the 243 test and library sources of the 2000 system.
status: inferred
from: 3.12 3.13 3.4.2.7
conf: mid
at: 2026-11-24

